            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, with almost 10 air changes per hour measured during testing. Air leakage was observed through the weather stripping around both doors, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). 



I also noted a significant amount of air leakage under your dryer. This could mean your dryer duct has come loose, or it may not be sealed well in that area. I recommend investigating further. There could also be air sealing potential around your furnace combustion air duct and under your electric hot water tank. 



Air leakage was also observed through your bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



Other upgrades listed in this report such as upgrading your furnace may also affect your air sealing. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 18% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $380&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

&lt;b&gt;Wall Insulation:&lt;/b&gt;



The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 3.5 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with 2 inches of XPS insulation (R10). I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



Insulation upgrades are typically done when siding needs to be replaced. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

&lt;b&gt;Crawlspaces:&lt;/b&gt; 



The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x10 joists with R40 insulation between the joists and 2 inches of XPS board insulation on the original section of your house, and just 2x10 joists with R40 insulation under your addition. I have modelled adding 2 inches of XPS board insulation to the floor of the addition to bring it up to the same R-value as the original house. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches rigid XPS insulation to the floor of your addition, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $10&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$0 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

&lt;b&gt;Windows:&lt;/b&gt;



I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Your windows are double paned and a mix of hinged and sliding windows, but they all appear to be in good physical shape. I do not recommend replacing them at this time. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 6 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $60&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Domestic Hot Water:&lt;/b&gt;



I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Oil Furnace:&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 86% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency propane fired furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a direct-vent furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether, which will also affect your air leakage. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;spend an additional $110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



The additional cost is based on May 2016 fuel prices. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

&lt;b&gt;Solar Upgrade:&lt;/b&gt;



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1010&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
